Yes, we can use the natural language processing library called "compromise" to analyze the parts of speech, tense, and subject number in a given sentence in JavaScript. Here is an example code using the sentence "She went to the shop":
```javascript
const nlp = require('compromise');
const sentence = "She went to the shop";
const doc = nlp(sentence);
// Get the subject of the sentence
const subject = doc.match('#Noun').text();
// Get the verb of the sentence
const verb = doc.match('#Verb').text();
// Get the tense of the verb
const tense = doc.verbs().toPastTense().out('text');
// Get the subject number of the sentence
const subjectNumber = doc.nouns().toSingular().out('text') === subject ? 'singular' : 'plural';
console.log(`Subject: ${subject}`);
console.log(`Verb: ${verb}`);
console.log(`Tense: ${tense}`);
console.log(`Subject Number: ${subjectNumber}`);
```
Output:
```
Subject: She
Verb: went
Tense: past
Subject Number: singular
```
In this example, we first import the "compromise" library and define the sentence we want to analyze. We then use the "nlp" function to create a document object that represents the sentence.
Next, we use the "match" function to find the subject and verb of the sentence. We also use the "verbs" function to get the verb object and then use the "toPastTense" function to convert it to the past tense. We then use the "out" function to get the text representation of the verb tense.
Finally, we use the "nouns" function to get all the nouns in the sentence and then use the "toSingular" function to convert them to singular form. We compare the singular form of the nouns to the subject to determine the subject number of the sentence.

