How can I use C# to help a farmer maximize his profit while staying within the time and land constraints? The farmer has 100 acres of land, and can only plant one crop per acre. Planting one acre of wheat yields $200 in profit and requires 2 hours of labor, while planting one acre of corn yields $300 in profit and requires 3 hours of labor. The farmer has 350 hours of labor available. What is the optimal number of acres he should plant with each crop?
Here's a C# code that can solve this problem:
int wheatProfit = 200;
int cornProfit = 300;
int wheatLabor = 2;
int cornLabor = 3;
int totalLand = 100;
int totalLabor = 350;
int wheatAcres = 0;
int cornAcres = 0;
int maxProfit = 0;
for (int i = 0; i <= totalLand; i++)
{
    int wheatAcresTemp = i;
    int cornAcresTemp = totalLand - i;
    int laborTemp = wheatLabor * wheatAcresTemp + cornLabor * cornAcresTemp;
    if (laborTemp <= totalLabor)
    {
        int profitTemp = wheatProfit * wheatAcresTemp + cornProfit * cornAcresTemp;
        if (profitTemp > maxProfit)
        {
            maxProfit = profitTemp;
            wheatAcres = wheatAcresTemp;
            cornAcres = cornAcresTemp;
        }
    }
}
