C++ can be used to implement psychological interventions in healthcare by creating software programs that incorporate evidence-based psychological techniques. For example, a program could be created to help patients manage stress and anxiety through relaxation techniques such as deep breathing and progressive muscle relaxation. The program could also include modules for cognitive-behavioral therapy, mindfulness, and other evidence-based interventions.
Here's a brief example of C++ code for a relaxation program:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>
using namespace std;
void deepBreathing() {
    cout << "Take a deep breath in through your nose, hold it for a few seconds, and exhale slowly through your mouth." << endl;
    this_thread::sleep_for(chrono::seconds(5)); // hold breath for 5 seconds
}
void progressiveMuscleRelaxation() {
    cout << "Tense the muscles in your arms and hold for a few seconds, then release and relax them completely." << endl;
    this_thread::sleep_for(chrono::seconds(5)); // hold tension for 5 seconds
    cout << "Tense the muscles in your legs and hold for a few seconds, then release and relax them completely." << endl;
    this_thread::sleep_for(chrono::seconds(5)); // hold tension for 5 seconds
}
int main() {
    string userInput;
    cout << "Welcome to the relaxation program. Press 'd' for deep breathing or 'p' for progressive muscle relaxation." << endl;
    cin >> userInput;
    if (userInput == "d") {
        deepBreathing();
    } else if (userInput == "p") {
        progressiveMuscleRelaxation();
    } else {
        cout << "Invalid input. Please try again." << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This program prompts the user to choose between deep breathing or progressive muscle relaxation, and then guides them through the chosen exercise with prompts and timed intervals. This is just a simple example, but it demonstrates how C++ can be used to create programs that incorporate psychological interventions for healthcare.

